    <main class="container">
        <article class="prose fade-in" style="padding-top: 6rem;">
            <h1>A guide to importing CAMS/CAS statements into a portfolio tracker</h1>
            <p>If you invest in mutual funds in India across various AMC platforms or brokers like Groww and Coin, the easiest way to see your entire transaction history is through a Consolidated Account Statement (CAS).</p>

            <h2>What is a CAS Statement?</h2>
            <p>A CAS (Consolidated Account Statement) is a single document that consolidates financial transactions across all your mutual funds and demat accounts based on your PAN number. It is securely issued by depository participants like CAMS or KFintech.</p>

            <h2>Step 1: Generate your CAS File</h2>
            <ol>
                <li>Visit the official CAMS Online portal (camsonline.com) or use their MFCentral app.</li>
                <li>Navigate to the "CAS / Statement" generation section.</li>
                <li>Select "Detailed" or "Summary" statement type (for a <a href="/portfolio-tracker-india/">portfolio tracker</a>, you usually need a 'Detailed' statement spanning 'Since Inception').</li>
                <li>Enter your registered email address and PAN.</li>
                <li>Set a secure password for the PDF file. You will receive an email shortly with your encrypted PDF.</li>
            </ol>

            <h2>Step 2: Import into Your Tracker</h2>
            <p>Once you have your PDF, importing it into a tracker like Arthavi is simple.</p>
            <ol>
                <li>Log in to your dashboard.</li>
                <li>Click on "Upload Investments" or "Sync Mutual Funds".</li>
                <li>Provide the encrypted PDF and occasionally the password you set (we handle parsing locally so your data remains strictly private).</li>
                <li>Watch as the tracker instantly reconstructs your historical buy/sell events and builds your real-time XIRR.</li>
            </ol>

            <h2>Troubleshooting CAS Import Issues</h2>
            <p>Sometimes, investors face issues when generating or uploading their CAS files. Here are common reasons why:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Incorrect Password:</strong> The most common error is typing the wrong password when unlocking the PDF. Always double-check your email or PAN-based password logic.</li>
                <li><strong>'Summary' instead of 'Detailed':</strong> Ensure you request a 'Detailed' transaction statement. Summary statements lack the specific transaction dates required to calculate an accurate XIRR.</li>
                <li><strong>Demat Holdings Missing:</strong> CAMS only tracks mutual funds held in regular folios. If you buy mutual funds in Demat form (like on Zerodha Coin), you must generate a CAS from NSDL or CDSL instead.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>How Often Should You Import CAS?</h2>
            <p>Unlike stocks which fluctuate wildly, mutual fund NAVs update only once a day. For most passive long-term investors, importing a new CAS statement once a month (after your SIPs trigger) is more than enough to keep their dashboard perfectly synced.</p>

            <p>Ditch the confusion of multiple accounts. Start organizing your investments securely. Explore the <a href="/portfolio-tracker-india/">best portfolio tracker in India</a> to upload your first CAS statement today.</p>
        </article>
    </main>

    
    

